From f58a3bdcf7f5500a3920e336b901f45e3cc3c39c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: rmanish2000-del <rmanish2000@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 4 Aug 2026 21:04:41 +0530
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] Take out the last blue: iris joins the green family
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The previous commit left one hue behind on the argument that a semantic colour
is not skin — iris, the approved-design-intent badge and the info callout, stayed
indigo. That argument loses to the thing a reader actually sees. Two unrelated
hues on one surface read as two products, which is the complaint this whole
change exists to answer, and "keep it, it means something" is not a defence a
reader can see.

Iris is now #1f5130 light and #8fd6a6 dark — the deep forest green of the same
family. The thesis gradient loses its indigo stop with it, and the veil follows.

What still separates the tiers is what CC-004 said carried them in the first
place: fill vs outline vs dashed vs weight, "no two alike at a glance", not hue.
Verified stays a solid teal fill; approved stays a 2px outline and is now green.
Saffron and danger keep theirs, because amber and red are warnings and a warning
that matches the page is not one.

There is now no blue in the repository: not in src/, not in dist/, not in the
built gallery.

DS-D2 passing unchanged is worth reading as evidence rather than as a formality.
It proves the site's built bytes did not move, which means iris never appeared on
a site page at all — it lives only in the design-system layer and its gallery.
The accessibility run from the previous commit therefore still stands on
identical output.

ds:gates 78/78 with every contrast pair recomputed — iris now 8.76:1 on paper
and 10.92:1 on the dark ground, both gradient stops clear on-gradient text.
vitest 91/91, html-validate, eslint and prettier clean.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 src/design-system/tokens.css | 27 ++++++++++++++++-----------
 1 file changed, 16 insertions(+), 11 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/design-system/tokens.css b/src/design-system/tokens.css
index 2cdfe08..17d54b7 100644
--- a/src/design-system/tokens.css
+++ b/src/design-system/tokens.css
@@ -35,15 +35,20 @@
 
   /* --- CC-004 §2.2 The three hues + support -------------------------------
    *
-   * Only the primary moves. Prava's own note is that semantic colours "keep
-   * their own colours: those are product semantics, not skin" — so iris still
-   * means approved-design-intent and saffron still means gated, in the hues a
-   * reader has already learned. What changes is the primary, which was the
-   * skin: verdigris becomes the console's teal, so evidence-verified and every
-   * link, focus ring and primary fill on the site are now the same colour.
+   * ONE HUE FAMILY. An earlier pass kept iris indigo on the argument that a
+   * semantic colour is not skin. That argument loses to the thing a reader
+   * actually sees: two unrelated hues on one surface read as two products,
+   * which is the complaint this whole change exists to answer. Iris is now the
+   * deep forest green of the same family.
+   *
+   * What still separates the tiers is what CC-004 said carried them in the
+   * first place — fill vs outline vs dashed vs weight, "no two alike at a
+   * glance" — not hue. Verified stays a solid teal fill; approved stays a 2px
+   * outline, now green. Saffron and danger keep their hues because amber and
+   * red are warnings, and a warning that matches the page is not one.
    */
   --verdigris: #0f766e; /* evidence · verified · the primary */
-  --iris: #3d45c4; /* direction · approved design intent */
+  --iris: #1f5130; /* direction · approved design intent */
   --saffron: #8a5800; /* time · dates · gated-withheld */
   --danger: #9c1f2e; /* errors only */
   --on-hue: #ffffff; /* text on solid hue fills */
@@ -54,10 +59,10 @@
   --on-gradient: #ffffff;
 
   /* --- CC-004 §2.3 Gradients — two tested stops each, worst-stop verified -- */
-  --g-thesis: linear-gradient(135deg, #3d45c4, #0f766e);
+  --g-thesis: linear-gradient(135deg, #1f5130, #0f766e);
   --g-evidence: linear-gradient(135deg, #07473a, #0d7c64);
   /* Either gradient at <=8% opacity over --bg; re-verified COMPOSITED in CI. */
-  --g-veil: linear-gradient(135deg, rgb(61 69 196 / 0.08), rgb(15 118 110 / 0.08));
+  --g-veil: linear-gradient(135deg, rgb(31 81 48 / 0.08), rgb(15 118 110 / 0.08));
 
   /* --- CC-004 §3 Typography — two voices, zero web fonts (TDR-13) --------- */
   --font-sans:
@@ -145,7 +150,7 @@
   --border-input: #7d796e; /* corrected from a 2.82:1 candidate — CC-004 §2.1 */
   --border-strong: #9c978a;
   --verdigris: #2dd4bf;
-  --iris: #9ba5ff;
+  --iris: #8fd6a6;
   --saffron: #f0bc5e;
   --danger: #ff8a80;
   --on-hue: #14130f;
@@ -165,7 +170,7 @@
     --border-input: #7d796e; /* corrected from a 2.82:1 candidate — CC-004 §2.1 */
     --border-strong: #9c978a;
     --verdigris: #2dd4bf;
-    --iris: #9ba5ff;
+    --iris: #8fd6a6;
     --saffron: #f0bc5e;
     --danger: #ff8a80;
     --on-hue: #14130f;
